    "answer": {
      "short": "For $k=2$, $RP=2b$ and $PQ=2c$. For $k=3$, $RP=3b$ and $PQ=3c$.\n\nConstruct parallel segments, use AAS congruence and opposite sides of a parallelogram, then apply the proved $k=2$ result within the $k=3$ construction.",
      "worked": "In each case, choose $F$ on $RQ$ with $RF=a$. Draw $FG\\parallel QP$, meeting $RP$ at $G$, and $FH\\parallel RP$, meeting $QP$ at $H$. Quadrilateral $FGPH$ is a parallelogram, so $HP=FG$ and $GP=FH$.\n\nBy corresponding angles, $\\angle GFR=\\angle ABC=\\beta$ and $\\angle GRF=\\angle ACB=\\gamma$. Also $FR=BC=a$. Therefore $\\triangle GFR\\cong\\triangle ABC$ by AAS, giving $RG=b$ and $FG=c$.\n\nFor $k=2$, $RQ=2a$, so $FQ=a$. By corresponding angles, $\\angle HQF=\\angle ABC=\\beta$ and $\\angle HFQ=\\angle ACB=\\gamma$. Together with $QF=BC=a$, AAS gives $\\triangle HQF\\cong\\triangle ABC$. Hence $FH=b$ and $QH=c$. Using opposite sides of the parallelogram,\n$$\\begin{align*}RP&=RG+GP\\\\RP&=b+FH\\\\RP&=2b,\\\\PQ&=QH+HP\\\\PQ&=c+FG\\\\PQ&=2c.\\end{align*}$$\nFor $k=3$, $RQ=3a$, so $FQ=2a$. The initial AAS proof again gives $RG=b$ and $FG=c$.\n\nThe triangles $HQF$ and $ABC$ are equiangular, with $H\\leftrightarrow A$, $Q\\leftrightarrow B$ and $F\\leftrightarrow C$, and $QF=2BC$. Apply the $k=2$ result just proved solely using congruence: $FH=2b$ and $QH=2c$. Therefore,\n$$\\begin{align*}RP&=RG+GP\\\\RP&=b+FH\\\\RP&=3b,\\\\PQ&=QH+HP\\\\PQ&=2c+FG\\\\PQ&=3c.\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
